The stock's fall to Rs 1187.55 marks a 20.5% drop from its 52-week high of Rs 1494.10, underscoring a significant correction over the past year. While the broader Sensex has also been under pressure, falling 3.63% over the last three weeks and trading close to its own 52-week low, the decline in ICICI Bank Ltd. has been more pronounced, with a 10.16% loss over the past year compared to the Sensex's 6.32% fall. The stock underperformed its sector as well, with the private banking segment down 2.04% on the day, while ICICI Bank Ltd. outperformed slightly by 0.32% in intraday trading.

Technically, the stock is trading below all major moving averages – 5-day, 20-day, 50-day, 100-day, and 200-day – signalling sustained downward momentum. The technical indicators paint a predominantly bearish picture, with the MACD and Bollinger Bands on weekly charts showing bearish signals, while monthly indicators suggest mild bearishness. The Relative Strength Index (RSI) remains neutral, offering no clear directional bias. What is driving such persistent weakness in ICICI Bank Ltd. when the broader market is in rally mode?

